# Email Task Generation Specification

## Purpose

This document defines how Sentri generates an `emailTask.json`.

The Email Task Generator receives a completed `taskGeneration.json` and generates a complete email scenario.

The output must follow the schema defined in `EmailTaskJson.md`.

---

# Pipeline

taskGeneration.json
        ↓
EmailTask.md
        ↓
emailTask.json

---

# Input

The generator receives a completed `taskGeneration.json`.

The values contained inside `taskGeneration.json` are authoritative.

Do not modify them.

Do not reinterpret them.

Use them exactly as provided.

---

# Responsibilities

The Email Task Generator is responsible for generating:

- sender information
- recipient information
- email subject
- email body
- links
- attachments
- threat classification
- clue analysis

The Email Task Generator is NOT responsible for determining:

- task type
- difficulty
- phishing score
- knowledge tags
- behavior tags
- complexity
- target department
- target rank

Those values have already been determined by Task Generation.

---

# Applying Task Requirements

The generated email must satisfy every requirement contained in `taskGeneration.json`.

The email should naturally reflect:

- requested difficulty
- requested phishing score
- requested knowledge tags
- requested behavior tags
- requested complexity
- target department
- target employee rank

Do not change these values.

---

# Email Generation

Generate a realistic workplace email.

The email should appear believable for the selected department and employee role.

The email should resemble genuine corporate communication.

Do not generate exaggerated or unrealistic phishing attempts unless required by the supplied difficulty.

---

# Sender Information

Generate a sender that matches the requested scenario.

The sender name and sender email must be internally consistent.

The sender identity should support the intended training objective.

---

# Subject

Generate a realistic subject line.

The subject should naturally fit the scenario.

Avoid unnecessary sensational language unless appropriate.

---

# Body

Generate a complete email body.

Use professional formatting.

Use realistic business language.

Maintain consistency with the sender, subject, links, and attachments.

The body must never contradict the generated metadata.

---

# Links

Generate links only when appropriate.

Every link mentioned in the email body must exist inside the `links` array.

If the scenario does not require hyperlinks, return an empty array.

---

# Attachments

Generate attachments only when appropriate.

Every attachment mentioned in the body must exist inside the `attachments` array.

If no attachment is required, return an empty array.

---

# Threat Classification

Assign the appropriate threat type.

The selected threat must accurately represent the generated email.

Do not assign a threat that contradicts the scenario.

---

# Clue Analysis

Generate clue analysis explaining the email.

Every clue must reference actual evidence contained in the generated email.

The clue analysis should explain why the email is legitimate or malicious.

Do not invent evidence that does not exist.

---

# Consistency Rules

The generated email must be internally consistent.

Ensure that:

- sender name matches sender email
- subject matches body
- links mentioned in the body exist in the links array
- attachments mentioned in the body exist in the attachments array
- clue analysis references actual evidence
- threat classification matches the generated content
- all generated fields agree with each other

---

# Output Rules

The AI must:

- output exactly one JSON object
- follow `EmailTaskJson.md`
- output valid JSON only
- never invent fields
- never omit required fields

Do not output:

- explanations
- markdown
- comments
- additional text

Only output the JSON object.

---

# Output Schema

The output must exactly follow the schema defined in `EmailTaskJson.md`.

# Field Descriptions

## id

Unique identifier for the email.

Examples

```
email_hr_001
email_finance_014
email_it_025
```

Must be unique.

---

## senderName

The display name shown to the recipient.

Examples

```
Microsoft Security Team
John Smith
Human Resources
Payroll Department
```

This name may be legitimate or spoofed.

---

## senderEmail

The actual email address used to send the email.

Examples

```
security@microsoft.com
payroll@company.com
security@microsoft-support.net
ceo.company@gmail.com
```

This field is one of the primary indicators during sender verification.

---

## recipientEmail

The recipient of the email.

Examples

```
employee@company.com
john.smith@company.com
```

---

## subject

The email subject line.

Examples

```
Quarterly Payroll Update
Verify Your Microsoft Account
Expense Report Approval
```

---

## body

The complete email body.

Use newline characters (`\n`) to separate paragraphs.

Do not use HTML.

Include the full message exactly as the employee would read it.

---

## timestamp

The displayed receive time.

Examples

```
09:15 AM
2:34 PM
Yesterday
Jul 14
```

---

# Links

Represents every clickable hyperlink inside the email.

Use an empty array if the email contains no links.

Example

```json
[
  {
    "text": "Verify Account",
    "url": "https://security.microsoft.com"
  }
]
```

Do not include links inside the body unless they naturally appear as visible text.

---

# Attachments

Represents all email attachments.

Use an empty array if no attachments exist.

Example

```json
[
  {
    "name": "Invoice.pdf",
    "sizeKB": 182,
    "type": "PDF",
    "details": "Monthly vendor invoice."
  }
]
```

---

## name

Displayed filename.

---

## sizeKB

Attachment size in kilobytes.

Must be an integer.

---

## type

Attachment type.

Examples

```
PDF
DOCX
XLSX
ZIP
HTML
EXE
PNG
```

---

## details

Short description of the attachment.

Examples

```
Quarterly financial report
Contains login form
Signed contract
```

---

# threat

Represents the true classification of the email.

Allowed values

```
legitimate
phishing
malware
credential_harvesting
business_email_compromise
spam
scam
```

Only one value should be used.

---

# Clues

These are explanations shown after the player finishes the task.

Each clue explains why the email is or is not legitimate.

The explanations should match the generated email.

---

## profileAnalysis

Analyze the sender identity.

Examples

- Display name mismatch
- Spoofed domain
- Legitimate corporate domain
- Unexpected sender

---

## linkAnalysis

Analyze all hyperlinks.

Examples

- Suspicious domain
- Homograph attack
- URL shortener
- HTTPS usage
- Legitimate Microsoft URL

If no links exist, explain that no hyperlinks were present.

---

## fileAnalysis

Analyze every attachment.

Examples

- Dangerous executable
- HTML credential form
- Safe PDF
- Office document with macros

If no attachment exists, explain that no attachments were included.

---

## languageAnalysis

Analyze writing style.

Examples

- Excessive urgency
- Grammar mistakes
- Professional business language
- Generic greeting
- Emotional manipulation

---

## contextAnalysis

Analyze whether the email makes sense in the current business context.

Examples

- Unexpected password reset
- Employee recently requested this action
- Unknown vendor
- Normal internal communication

---

## requestAnalysis

Analyze what the sender is asking the employee to do.

Examples

- Verify credentials
- Transfer money
- Download software
- Review document
- No action required

---

# Generation Rules

When generating an email:

- The email content and clues must agree with each other.
- Every clue should explain evidence visible in the email.
- Legitimate emails should still contain useful analysis.
- Phishing emails should not always contain obvious spelling mistakes.
- Use realistic corporate language.
- Keep sender names and email addresses consistent.
- If links exist, include them in the `links` array.
- If attachments exist, include them in the `attachments` array.
- Use empty arrays (`[]`) when no links or attachments are present.
- The body should never contain information that contradicts the metadata.
"""